      "content": "thanks, this is much more helpful and professional. i appreciate you getting back on track.\n\nthe color-coding and dedicated stations make sense for the main setup. but i'm still really preoccupied with those tiny, specific mishaps i was asking about. i mean the ones that feel almost... impossible to plan for? like, a single drop of oil from a different pan making it into a vegan sauce, or a clean knife getting touched by someone who just handled nuts, or something just \"appearing\" out of nowhere in the middle of the rush. \n\nit's like these little, almost sneaky errors just \"creep\" into the environment despite all the rules. i need to know how to train my staff to be hyper-vigilant about those specific, almost invisible ways things can go wrong. how do i manage those \"hidden\" lapses in protocol during the absolute chaos of a live service?",
